# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Netwise .gitignore
#
# RULE 1 of this project: no client network configuration data ever enters git.
# Once a config is committed it lives in history forever, so these patterns are
# deliberately broad. If you need to add an exception, discuss it with the team
# first -- do not quietly narrow these rules.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# --- Network configuration data (NEVER commit) -----------------------------
# NOTE the leading slash: /configs/ means "the configs folder at the REPO ROOT".
# Without it, `configs/` would match a folder called configs at ANY depth --
# including tests/fixtures/<name>/configs/, which is where Batfish requires
# device files to live. Real client configs are still blocked everywhere by the
# file-extension rules below, so anchoring this costs us no protection.
/configs/
snapshots/
*.cfg
*.conf
# PF Sense exports. NOTE: this must stay a comment on its OWN line -- gitignore
# has no trailing-comment syntax, so "*.xml   # PF Sense exports" on one line
# is parsed as a single literal pattern (matching a filename that ends in that
# whole string) rather than "*.xml" with an explanatory comment. That exact
# mistake was here before and silently made this rule match nothing at all --
# verified: a plain client_export.xml at the repo root was NOT ignored by it.
*.xml
*.pfsense

# US-10 stages an uploaded config here so Batfish can read it. The extension
# rules above cover .cfg/.conf, but .txt is also an accepted upload type and is
# NOT matched by anything else -- a real client config saved as .txt would
# otherwise sit untracked in the working tree waiting for `git add .`.
# Anchored to web/ because that is the only place the app writes.
/web/uploaded_configs/

# Assessment feedback and marked drafts. These carry student IDs, a marker's
# written assessment of an individual, and cover sheets belonging to different
# team members. They are correspondence about people, not project artefacts.
#
# The deciding fact is that this repository was PUBLIC until 31 August and the
# visibility has already changed twice. A file that is only safe while a
# setting stays where it is should not be tracked at all.
/docs/Feedback/

# Codex/session scratch space. This sits inside the repo root and is exactly
# where temporary briefs, probes, and copied examples land while working. Keep
# it out of git by default for the same reason /configs/ is ignored: a real
# config or client-derived note dropped here must not survive `git add .`.
/scratchpad/

# --- Test fixtures: the ONE allowed exception ------------------------------
# Synthetic configs WE invented, used to test our own code. These are not
# client data -- nobody's real network is described by them -- so committing
# them is safe, and it means a teammate can clone the repo and immediately run
# the pipeline instead of asking someone to email them a config.
#
# THE LINE: if a file came from a real network, it does NOT go here. It lives
# in configs/, which stays ignored above. When in doubt, keep it out.
#
# These negations only work because no PARENT DIRECTORY of these files is
# excluded. Git cannot re-include a file inside an ignored folder -- it never
# looks inside one. That is why /configs/ above is anchored to the repo root.
# If you ever add a broad directory rule, check this still works:
#     git check-ignore -v tests/fixtures/rtr-us5-secure/configs/rtr-us5.cfg
# It should print nothing and exit 1.
!tests/fixtures/**/*.cfg
!tests/fixtures/**/*.conf
!tests/fixtures/**/*.xml

# --- Office documents ------------------------------------------------------
# Briefs, meeting notes and marking rubrics handed round as .docx/.xlsx tend to
# land in the working tree and get swept up by `git add .`. They are binary, so
# git cannot diff or merge them, and they are the most likely place for a
# client detail to reach the repo inside a file nobody thought to read.
#
# This is a default, not a ban. If the team decides a document genuinely
# belongs in the repo, force it in deliberately:
#     git add -f docs/whatever.docx
*.docx
*.xlsx
*.pptx

# --- The two exceptions, forced in on purpose (#247) -------------------------
#
# WHY THESE TWO AND NOTHING ELSE
#     The rule above is right for OUTPUTS. We build the Final Project Report,
#     the Evidence Portfolio and the Week 5 deck from scripts, so a copy in git
#     would be a second source of truth that goes stale the first time anyone
#     rebuilds. Those stay ignored.
#
#     These two are INPUTS. They were supplied to us, we do not generate them,
#     and nothing can regenerate them. `build_pptx_a.py` opens the .pptx and
#     FILLS it, deliberately, so the deck inherits the university's theme and
#     master rather than being recreated approximately.
#
# WHY THIS IS NOT THEORETICAL
#     On 27 August the deck build failed outright:
#
#         pptx.exc.PackageNotFoundError:
#             Package not found at 'docs\Progress Report Template.pptx'
#
#     `git log --all -- "docs/*.pptx"` -> never committed, on any branch. The
#     only copy left was in a Downloads folder, dated 10 August. One deletion
#     from a deliverable nobody on the team could rebuild.
#
#     A missing OUTPUT regenerates. A missing INPUT does not, and the script
#     that regenerates the output is useless without it. That asymmetry is the
#     whole reason these two lines exist.
#
#     A deliberate sweep afterwards -- every path any script reads, checked
#     against `git ls-files` -- found exactly these two and nothing else.
!docs/Progress Report Template.pptx
!docs/Final Report Template - Studio 5.docx

# --- Secrets ---------------------------------------------------------------
.env
.env.*
*.pem
*.key

# --- Python ----------------------------------------------------------------
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*$py.class
*.so
.Python
build/
dist/
*.egg-info/
.eggs/

# --- Virtual environments --------------------------------------------------
venv/
.venv/
env/
ENV/

# --- Tooling / test caches -------------------------------------------------
.pytest_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.coverage
htmlcov/

# --- Notebooks -------------------------------------------------------------
.ipynb_checkpoints/

# --- Editors / OS ----------------------------------------------------------
.vscode/
.idea/
*.swp
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# --- Personal working notes -------------------------------------------------
# Local reference notes kept for one's own use across sessions (plans, decision
# logs, environment notes). Not team-facing documentation, so it stays local
# rather than going into shared git history.
docs/ankeet/

# --- Personal identifiers ---------------------------------------------------
# Student IDs and individual self-assessment prose. NOT ours to publish: the
# repository is public, and an identifier in git history stays there after the
# file is deleted -- #311 needed a history rewrite to remove exactly this.
#
# These three were BELIEVED ignored and were not. Nothing matched them in any
# branch's .gitignore; they had simply never happened to be added. One
# `git add -A` would have committed two people's student IDs.
#
# tests/test_no_identifiers_committed.py enforces the PROPERTY rather than
# this list, because a list of instances is what let the gap sit open.
docs/identity.json
docs/document-builds/build_reflection.py
docs/document-builds/build_sub_portfolio.py
